<header><h1>Space Combat: Proof</h1></header>
<article>
<p>The source text behind each note on <a href="/R_space_combat">Space Combat</a>. <b>Explicit</b> sources state the note directly. <b>Implicit</b> sources support it without stating it.</p>

<h2>Notes</h2>
    <ol class="note">
    <li>Abilities that resolve at the start of a space combat, such as the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/Warfare_Technologies#Assault_Cannon"><i>Assault Cannon</i></a> technology and the <a href="/F_mentak">Mentak</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Mentak_Coalition#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Ambush"><sc>Ambush</sc></a> faction ability, are resolved before the <b>Anti&ndash;Fighter Barrage</b> step.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>5</sup> Designer ruling: &ldquo;Assault Cannon and Mentak&rsquo;s Ambush happen before Anti-Fighter Barrage, which is its own distinct timing (essentially the first step of combat).&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Mentak&rsquo;s Ambush work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;At the start of space combat, before Anti-Fighter Barrage.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Abilities that trigger &ldquo;at the start of a combat&rdquo;, other than those on agents, can only be resolved during a combat in which the player resolving them is participating.<sup>4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>4</sup> Designer ruling: &ldquo;Can abilities that trigger &lsquo;at the start of a combat&rsquo; be played in combats you are not participating in? A: No, any non-agent &lsquo;at the start of a combat&rsquo; abilities must be played during a combat you are participating in.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player&rsquo;s fighters and ground forces do not count against capacity during combat. At the end of combat, any excess units are removed and returned to that player&rsquo;s reinforcements.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR 16.4c: &ldquo;A player&rsquo;s fighters and ground forces do not count against capacity during combat. At the end of combat, any excess units are removed and returned to that player&rsquo;s reinforcements.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is capacity checked if a carrier is lost?&rdquo;: &ldquo;During combat fighters and ground forces may exceed capacity and keep fighting; the excess is removed when the space combat ends.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>5</sup> Designer ruling (Milty, 08/02/2018): &ldquo;During a combat, the fighters would persist until the end of combat&rdquo; Fighters persist through combat despite capacity.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Capacity is checked after the winner of a space combat is determined.<sup>1</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR 78.10a: &ldquo;If the winner of the combat has fighters or ground forces in the space area&rdquo; over capacity, excess units are removed then.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <ol><li>It is possible to win a space combat with only fighters remaining, before those fighters are removed due to lack of capacity.<sup>1</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR 78.10a: &ldquo;If the winner of the combat has fighters or ground forces in the space area of the active system and those units exceed the capacity of that player&rsquo;s ships in that system, that player must choose and remove any excess units.&rdquo; The winner is decided before the excess is removed.</li>
        </ul></li></ol>
    <li>The following apply to retreats:</li>
    <ol>
        <li>Ships that have announced a retreat still roll dice during the Roll Dice step of that combat round, then retreat during the Retreat step. Retreating this way avoids abilities that resolve after the combat round, such as the <a href="/F_norr">N&rsquo;orr</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/Sardakk_N&#x27;orr#:~:text=Exotrireme"><i>Exotrireme II</i></a>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do ships that announced a retreat still roll that round?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; announcing a retreat doesn&rsquo;t stop you firing; you roll normally and then leave in the retreat step. Retreating in that step also escapes Exotrireme II.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>If a space combat reaches a state where neither player can win, the attacker must retreat. If the attacker cannot retreat, their units in that combat are destroyed.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens if neither side can ever win a space combat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The Living Rules Reference FAQ says the attacker must retreat. If the attacker has nowhere legal to go, players generally treat those ships as destroyed.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR FAQ: &ldquo;If neither side has the potential to win, the attacker must retreat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>Modifying the move value of a ship will have no effect when retreating. Retreats are limited to adjacent systems.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Where can my ships retreat to?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Movement bonuses don&rsquo;t matter, as a retreat always goes to an adjacent system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>The <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/Propulsion_Technologies#Dark_Energy_Tap"><i>Dark Energy Tap</i></a> technology allows a player to retreat into a system that does not contain their ships or a planet they control.<sup>2,8</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>8</sup> PoK rulebook, Dark Energy Tap: &ldquo;Your ships can retreat into adjacent systems that do not contain other players&rsquo; units, even if you do not have units or control planets in that system.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Where can my ships retreat to?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Dark Energy Tap drops the second requirement.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>Ships can retreat into a <a href="/R_nebula">nebula</a>, as a nebula is intended to no longer need to be the active system for units to move into it.<sup>4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>4</sup> Designer ruling: &ldquo;Nebula rules will be changed. A Nebula will no longer require being the active system in order to move into it.&rdquo; A retreat moves ships into an adjacent system that is not the active system.</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>A player can retreat into an <a href="/R_asteroid_field">asteroid field</a> or <a href="/R_supernova">supernova</a> only if an effect allows their ships to move into it, such as the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/Propulsion_Technologies#Antimass_Deflectors"><i>Antimass Deflectors</i></a> technology or the <a href="/F_muaat">Muaat</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Embers_of_Muaat#Faction_Technologies:~:text=Magmus%20Reactor"><i>Magmus Reactor</i></a> technology.<sup>*</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Unverified</b>: No source found. LRR 78.7 retreat movement combined with the asteroid field and supernova movement rules and the card text.</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>Ships retreating out of a gravity rift are affected by it; see <a href="/R_gravity_rift">Gravity Rift</a>.<sup>1</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR 41.2: &ldquo;For each ship that would move out of or through a gravity rift, one die is rolled immediately before it exits the gravity rift system.&rdquo; A retreat moves ships out of the active system.</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>The <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/Action_Cards#Codex_I:~:text=Nav%20Suite"><i>Nav Suite</i></a> action card will not allow a player to ignore an anomaly while retreating, as a retreat does not happen during the <b>Movement</b> step.<sup>*</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Unverified</b>: No source found.</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>Ships can retreat out of the active system, and transport units out of it, even if it contains the retreating player&rsquo;s command token.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I retreat or Skilled Retreat into, or carry units out of, systems that contain my command token?&rdquo;: &ldquo;retreating ships can carry units out of the combat system even if your token is there, because the transport restriction excludes the active system, whoever activated it.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR, Space Combat, Retreat step: &ldquo;To retreat, a player takes all of their ships with a move value in the combat and moves them to a single system that is adjacent to the active system.&rdquo; No command token restriction applies.</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>While retreating, a player may transport zero or more ground forces from planets they control in the active system. The player need not decide which ground forces they transport until they execute the retreat.<sup>2,10</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>10</sup> Codex I: &ldquo;when a ship moves as part of a retreat, it can transport ground forces that are on planets in its system as normal.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When my ships retreat, can they pick up ground forces from my planets in the combat system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;retreating ships move and transport units like any other movement.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>While retreating, a player cannot transport more fighters and/or ground forces than they have capacity in the retreating ships.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR 78.7b: &ldquo;That player&rsquo;s fighters and ground forces in the space area of the active system that are unable to move or be transported are removed.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>5</sup> Designer ruling: &ldquo;the number you can attempt to move out of the system is still limited by your ships capacity in the same way that it is when you move.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Skilled Retreat differ &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the usual transport rules apply.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>When a player retreats, their fighters that remain in the space area of the active system are removed, even if that system contains one of their space docks.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens to units that can&rsquo;t go with a retreat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Fighters and ground forces in the space area that the retreating ships can&rsquo;t carry are destroyed, even if a space dock could have supported them.&rdquo; RulesPal says destroyed; LRR 78.7b says removed.</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>5</sup> Designer ruling (sigmazero13, Jul 18, 2018): &ldquo;They&rsquo;d be destroyed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>Ground forces transported by retreating ships, or moved with the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/Action_Cards#Twilight_Imperium_Fourth_Edition:~:text=Skilled%20Retreat"><i>Skilled Retreat</i></a> action card, cannot land on planets in the system they move to; they remain in the space area.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can ground forces that retreated with my ships, or moved with Skilled Retreat, land on a planet in the new system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; landing only happens in the invasion step of a tactical action in the active system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    </ol>
    <li>A player only wins a combat if a space combat or ground combat occurred. Destroying all opposing ships with the Space Cannon ability, destroying all opposing ground forces with the Bombardment ability, landing ground forces on a planet without opposing ground forces, or the opposing player avoiding the combat with the <a href="/F_naalu">Naalu</a> player&rsquo;s Foresight faction ability does not count as winning a combat. A planet containing only another player&rsquo;s structures does not cause a ground combat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Space Cannon, Bombardment or landing on an empty planet count as winning a combat for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; a combat is a space combat or a ground combat &hellip; Destroying ships with Space Cannon during movement, bombarding away all defenders, or landing unopposed involves no combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The &ldquo;0&rdquo; side of the d10 represents a result of 10.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR FAQ: &ldquo;Yes, the &lsquo;0&rsquo; face is a &lsquo;10&rsquo; result.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the 0 on the ten-sided die count as 0 or 10?&rdquo;: &ldquo;10.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    </ol>
</article>
